## [ENT-129] | 2026-11-22 08:27 UTC | Apophis | error | ref: skill military-turn group_hunt.py | see: orders.log ENT-062
What: the whale group of three Pulse Laser III drones (group_hunt.py, started 08:07 UTC) never fought: one of its pilots, taken at 08:07 UTC with no order file, still flew an engine goal (navigate-to-system toward Ironpeak, the ship file at Unknown Edge 08:28 UTC); its dock goal was refused at 08:23 UTC three times ("A goal is already executing on this account") and the parallel dock step stalled. I stopped the script at 08:29 UTC and released all three holds (gate.py release).
Effect: three drones held by hand for 7 to 22 minutes without work; no shot, no hull lost, no credit lost.
Cause: take_free counted a pilot free when it had no order and its ship file stood within --max-jumps; it never read whether the engine still ran a goal, and a ship in flight reads a stale system.
Fix: group_hunt.py take_free skips a pilot whose ship file shows a goal type (skill script, 2026-11-22 08:30 UTC); the group runs again with it.
Class: a ship counted free by one field (its order) while another (its engine goal) says busy: the same class as the runner-hold race of 2026-11-21. Would it have prevented this one: yes, the pilot's ship file read goal navigate-to-system at the take.

## [ENT-130] | 2026-11-22 08:33 UTC | Apophis | error | ref: skill military-turn stop_script.sh | see: ENT-129
What: two programs drove one pilot (rulebook 8.3). To stop the stalled whale group (ENT-129) I sent kill -9 to the PID the terminal had returned for the background job: that was the shell wrapper, and the python process of group_hunt.py lived on. After I had released its three holds, it went on: its pilot that the dispatcher had already given a hunt (order since 08:30:43 UTC) created a game fleet, invited two pilots that a second group run of mine now held (both refused), set combat mode and tried navigate goals beside the runner until I killed the python process at about 08:31:30 UTC (ps, PID by its command line). Correction to ENT-129: its times 08:29 and 08:30 UTC are wrong; that entry was committed 08:27:52 UTC, the script stopped before.
Effect: no hull, credit or ship lost (the runner's hunt went on); the pilot leads a game fleet of one and its default battle stance may be fire (the first script sets fire before the fleet step); set_stance.py runs to set it back to flee when it docks idle.
Cause: I stopped a process by a PID I had not read from ps, and released the holds without checking that no process of the script ran.
Fix: skill script stop_script.sh: finds the script's python processes by command line, SIGINT, then SIGKILL, checks none is left, and only then releases the pilots; it releases nothing while one survives. The skill names it as the only way to stop a background script.
Class: an action on a process taken from an unverified handle (the same class as a hash written before the watcher rebased it). Would it have prevented this one: yes, ps showed the python PID 44541 beside the wrapper 44537.

