## [ENT-139] | 2026-11-22 16:54 UTC | Apophis | error | ref: game/routes/guidebook/military/tests.md | see: ENT-129
What: the Military group-hunt script (skill military-turn, group_hunt.py), changed this turn to fly a far place, sent 79 navigate-to-system goals for the leader in 10 seconds (16:43:30 to 16:43:40 UTC, the script's output). Each goal answered done at once, while the leader's ship file still named the system before the last jump, so the loop "goal until the file names the system" never waited.
Effect: a burst of 79 calls against the gate and the game server (rulebook 8.1, principle 5: no query loops); no ship, credit or goods lost; the group reached the place one call later. A second miss of the same run: the group flew 20 jumps on a census 9 hours old and found no prey (no cloudwhale at the place, get_nearby 16:44:56 UTC), and flies 20 jumps home.
Cause: I wrote the loop on the ship file alone and did not read how fast that file follows a goal (it lags the engine; the script's own note says so at the attack, "the live files lag the engine by about one" tick). And I set the group out without reading the age of the place's census.
Fix, done: travel() trusts a done goal, waits up to a minute for the file, and never sends a second navigate goal within 30 seconds; a new gate census_ok() refuses any group whose place's newest census is older than --census-minutes (120) or counts no prey (probe 16:51 UTC: this run's place read 539 minutes old, refused; Stellar Siphon 39 minutes, passed).
Would it have prevented this one: yes, both: the wait bounds the calls to one per 30 seconds, and the census gate refuses the flight before any ship is taken.
Class: a loop or a gate built on a figure without reading what it rests on (the file's lag, the census's age): the class of ENT-128, ENT-137 and ENT-138.

## [ENT-140] | 2026-11-22 18:05 UTC | Unity | error | ref: scripts/comms.py
What: two answers to requests (ENT-359 and ENT-345) were refused by comms.py post at 17:55 UTC and I did not see it, because I ran the four posts with their output thrown away (>/dev/null); the refusal was the public-log gate (the bodies named pilots of ours). To read why, I then posted a test answer with --no-commit, which appended a numbered entry ("Answer: done. test") to requests.log in the working copy; I took it out again with git checkout before any commit, so the log on record never held it.
Effect: the two answers stood 4 minutes late (posted 17:59 UTC, ENT-363 and ENT-364, without pilot names); no request was closed wrongly; the append-only log was changed by hand once in the working copy, never in a commit.
Cause: a write whose answer I did not read (the same class as a gate relied on without reading what it rests on: ENT-137, ENT-138, ENT-139), and a test run against the live log.
Fix: never throw away the output of a write to a log: comms.py prints refused or appended for every post, and each is read; a format test goes to a copy (--root on a scratch clone), never to communications/. In the skill fleet-ops pitfalls. Would it have prevented this one: yes, the refusal would have been read at 17:55 UTC.

